    {
     "owner": {
      "account_id": 68410,
      "reputation": 20690,
      "user_id": 199607,
      "user_type": "registered",
      "accept_rate": 87,
      "profile_image": "https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/607564f1a15f1899c71b4a9fdfc33ac2?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG",
      "display_name": "Simon Perepelitsa",
      "link": "https://stackoverflow.com/users/199607/simon-perepelitsa"
     },
     "is_accepted": true,
     "score": 0,
     "last_activity_date": 1653668388,
     "creation_date": 1653668388,
     "answer_id": 72408345,
     "question_id": 72406800,
     "content_license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
     "body": "<p>You can run the same queries on different schemas just by changing <code>search_path</code> variable.</p>\n<pre><code>set search_path = schema1;\nselect * from orders; -- resolves to schema1.orders\n\nset search_path = schema2;\nselect * from orders; -- resolves to schema2.orders\n</code></pre>\n<p>As for views, you will have create/drop them individually across all schemas just like you do with the tables.</p>\n"
    }
   ],
